  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 AM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nAn upper-level ridge has moved over SD and it will stick around over \nthe areas through Thursday. It is helping to support southerly winds \nmoving warm temperatures into central and northeastern SD. \nTemperatures will be between 10-20 degrees warmer than normal today \nand Wednesday, with highs in the upper 60s to mid 70s. There is a \nsmall shortwave aloft that will move along the contours of the \nridge. This will push a weak low pressure and front through central \nand northeastern SD tonight and Wednesday morning. Luckily, there is \ndrier air over SD, which will stop the front from developing \nprecipitation. The only thing one would notice from the front moving \nthrough will be a change in the wind direction. As dry air sticks \naround today and on Wednesday, there will be sunny to mostly sunny \nskies.\n\nThings will start to change Thursday, as an upper-level trough helps \nto push a surface low pressure from NV to the northeast over MT and \nthen Saskatchewan. While this low pressure will not move over SD, it \nwill have a couple of fronts that move over the state. Additionally, \nit will cause the pressure gradient to tighten over SD as the low \npressure interacts with a strong high pressure over the Great Lakes. \nThis tight pressure gradient will start to strengthen the winds \naloft that will then mix down to the surface, causing strong \nsoutherly wind gusts to occur. The strongest of these wind gusts \nwill occur Friday afternoon through Saturday morning. Ensembles show \nthat there is a 30-50% chance for the winds to gust up to and over \nthan 45 mph. These winds will need to be watched in upcoming model \nruns to see if a wind headline might be needed. These southerly \nwinds will help to push more warm air into SD, which will increase \nthe temperatures. The temperatures will be 15-25 degrees warmer than \nnormal Thursday and Friday, with highs getting up into the 70s to \nlow 80s. The warmest temperatures will be over south central SD, \nwith a 40-60% chance that they get over 80 degrees. \n\nThe winds are not the only thing that will need to be watched in \nupcoming model runs. While this low pressure system will bring our \nnext chance for rain, the models still vary the timing, track, and \namounts. The models agree that the warm front for this low will be \nmoving over SD Thursday and stay over the state into Friday. The \nmodels vary when and where rain and potentially storms start to \ndevelop along this front and how quickly the front (and its rain) \nmoves out. The models then vary when the cold front moves in, when \nthe rain might develop along it, as well as how long the front and \nrain stays over portions of northeastern SD Saturday into Sunday. \nThere is still a 30-50% chance of precipitation occurring a some \npoint Friday and at sometime on Saturday, there is just lower \nconfidence on when and where.",
